      "answer": "$T=106.97\\,\\mathrm{N}$ and $\\theta=37.5^\\circ$.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "EITHER:\n$T \\sin \\theta  + 120 \\sin 45^\\circ = 15g$ — M1 for an attempt to resolve (either direction with the correct number of terms and dimensionally correct); A1 for resolving vertically.\n$T \\cos \\theta  = 120 \\cos 45^\\circ$ — A1 for resolving horizontally.\n$\\tan \\theta  = (15g - 120 \\sin 45^\\circ)/(120 \\cos 45^\\circ)$ or $T = \\sqrt{65.15^2+84.85^2}$ — M1 for using division to find the angle or a right-triangle relation to find the force magnitude.\n$\\theta  = 37.5^\\circ$ — A1. $T = 107\\,\\mathrm{N}$ — A1.\n\nOR1: $120/\\sin(90^\\circ + \\theta ) = T/\\sin 135^\\circ = 15g/\\sin(135^\\circ - \\theta )$ — A1 one correct equation; A1 a second correct equation; M1 attempt to solve for the angle or tension; $\\theta  = 37.5^\\circ$ — A1; $T = 107\\,\\mathrm{N}$ — A1; M1 attempt to use a triangle of forces.\n\nOR2: $T/\\sin 45^\\circ = 15g/\\sin(45^\\circ + \\theta ) = 120/\\sin(90^\\circ - \\theta )$ — A1 one correct equation; A1 a second correct equation; M1 attempt to solve for the angle or tension; $\\theta  = 37.5^\\circ$ — A1; $T = 107\\,\\mathrm{N}$ — A1; M1 attempt to use a triangle of forces.\n\nOR3: $T^2 = 150^2 + 120^2 - 2(150)(120) \\cos 45^\\circ$ — M1, use of the cosine rule in a triangle with sides 120, 150 and T and corresponding angles $90^\\circ - \\theta $, $45^\\circ + \\theta $, $45^\\circ$; A1 correct equation. $T = 107\\,\\mathrm{N}$ — A1. M1 use of sine rule or cosine rule in an attempt to find the angle. $120/\\sin(90^\\circ - \\theta ) = 106.97/\\sin 45^\\circ$ — A1, a correct equation in the angle such as this. $\\theta  = 37.5^\\circ$ — A1.\n\nPrinted total: 6.",
